  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1041 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nSurface high pressure will continue to build into the Mid-South\novernight in the wake of today's cold frontal passage, setting\nthe stage for a noticeably cooler and drier weekend across the\nregion. The gusty winds that occurred behind the front will\ndiminish some overnight as the pressure gradient relaxes. This\ninflux of cooler, drier air will drop overnight low temperatures\ninto the low to mid-40s, bringing a crisp start to the weekend.\nBelow normal temperatures will be common across the Mid-South\nSaturday with highs in the 60s as skies gradually clear.\n\nThe primary concern in the short term will be the potential for\npatchy frost as the center of the surface high settles in\nSaturday night into Sunday morning. With clear skies, nearly calm\nwinds, and a very dry airmass in place, efficient radiational\ncooling will allow temperatures to plunge into the upper 30s to\nnear 40 degrees across the Mid-South. HREF probabilities paint a\n40 to 60 percent chance of temperatures falling to less than\n37 degrees across parts of West Tennessee, the Missouri,\nBootheel, and eastern Arkansas.\n\nLooking ahead to next week, temperatures will gradually warm. The\nwork week will begin with high temperatures in the upper 60s to\nmiddle 70s, warming slightly each day with upper 70s to mid/upper\n80s by Thursday and Friday. No precipitation is expected over the\nnext 7 days.",
